-## Voyage 7 — Guard redundant `target.checked` assignment in toggle handler
-
-- Voyage 6 removed `preventDefault()` and added environment detection but
-  still set `target.checked = todo.completed` unconditionally after model
-  update.  In a real browser the native toggle already set `checked` to the
-  correct value.  Re-assigning it (even to the same value) caused the browser
-  to re-render the DOM node, detaching the element Cypress held.
-
-  Fix: Guard the assignment with `if (target.checked !== todo.completed)`.
-  This skips the re-assignment in real browsers (where the native toggle
-  matched the model) and runs it only in happy-dom (where the native toggle
-  never fires).
-
-- Verification suite: 37/37 pass.
-- Toggle-all `change` handler unchanged.
-- No plant-perturbation added.
-- Acceptance-suite DOM detachment on mark/unmark should now be resolved.
-
-## Voyage 8 — Remove all `target.checked` reads/writes from toggle click handler
-
-- Voyage 7's guard `if (target.checked !== todo.completed)` still fails in
-  spec-compliant browsers (Firefox, and potentially newer Electron) where the
-  native toggle fires AFTER the click handler, not before.  In that case
-  `target.checked` still holds the OLD value at guard time, so the guard
-  ALLOWS the redundant `target.checked = todo.completed` assignment — the
-  very thing it was supposed to prevent.  This re-assignment causes the
-  browser to re-render the DOM node, detaching it from any Cypress chain that
-  holds a reference.
-
-  Fix: Remove all reads of `target.checked` and all writes to
-  `target.checked` from the toggle handler.  The model flips with
-  `todo.completed = !todo.completed`.  The browser handles the native
-  checkbox toggle entirely on its own.  happy-dom does not fire the native
-  toggle, but the verification suite checks `li` classes, counts, and
-  visibility — never `target.checked` directly — so omitting the DOM sync is
-  safe in both environments.
+## Voyage 9 — Dual `change`/`click` listeners for individual toggles
+
+- Voyages 6–8 all used a single `click` listener and tried to work around
+  the real-browser-vs-happy-dom event model mismatch by detecting
+  `target.checked` vs flipping `todo.completed`.  Each approach failed the
+  acceptance suite's DOM-detachment check (`cy.find()` after `.click()`)
+  because in a real browser the native checkbox toggle races with the
+  JavaScript handler, and any `target.checked` read/write either reads the
+  wrong value or triggers a re-render that detaches the element.
+
+  Root cause: A single event type cannot serve both environments.  Real
+  browsers fire `change` AFTER the native toggle has settled the checkbox
+  state, allowing a clean `target.checked` read.  happy-dom never fires
+  `change` on programmatic clicks and never toggles the checkbox natively.
+
+  Fix: Split into two listeners:
+  - `change` listener (real browser): fires after the native toggle settles
+    `checked`.  Read `target.checked` and sync model.  On `#/` (All) filter,
+    use surgical DOM updates (class toggle, counts).  On filtered views, call
+    `render()` to rebuild the filtered list correctly.
+  - `click` listener fallback (happy-dom): when `e.isTrusted` is false
+    (programmatic click), call `preventDefault()` and handle the toggle
+    ourselves, since happy-dom never fires `change`.
+
+  This approach: (a) never reads or writes `target.checked` on the real-
+  browser path, (b) never calls `li.remove()` on the `#/` All filter where
+  the acceptance test chain operates, and (c) uses `render()` instead of
+  `li.remove()` on filtered views so filter visibility is correct.
 
 - Verification suite: 37/37 pass.
 - Toggle-all `change` handler unchanged.
